\date{2026-03-30T13:28:26Z}
\tag{discrete}
\taxon{Example}

\import{discrete-macros}

\title{Growth of #{2^n} and #{n!}}

\p{As a second example, consider the predicate #{P(n)} defined as #{2^n < n!}. If we try to prove this for all #{n \in \N}, we quickly discover that it isn’t: #{P(0)} is not true (#{2^0 = 1} which is not less than #{0! = 1}); neither is #{P(1)} (#{2^1} is not less than #{1!}), nor #{P(2)}, nor #{P(3)}. However, all is not lost: #{P(4)} is true (#{2^4 = 16 < 4! = 24}), and we suspect that #{P(n)} is true from then on, that is, for all #{n \geq 4}. We can still use induction to prove this; we just need to use a different base case.  Formally, as stated, the [principle of induction](00CC) does not allow this; but it still works in the same way to prove that something is true for all natural numbers #{n \geq 4} instead of #{n \geq 0}.  If we wanted to be completely formal, we could define #{Q(n) = P(n+4)} and then prove #{Q(n)} for all #{n \geq 0}, which amounts to the same thing.}

\transclude{00CK}
\proof{
By induction on #{n}.
\subproof{
  In the base case, #{2^4 < 4!} is true.
}
\subproof{
  Now let #{k} be an arbitrary natural number such that #{k \geq 4}. Suppose #{P(k)} is true, that is, ##{2^k < k!.}
We wish to show that #{2^{k+1} < (k+1)!} as well.

\stmt{2^{k+1}}
\reason{=}{algebra}
\stmt{2 \cdot 2^k}
\reason{<}{induction hypothesis; if #{a < b} then #{2a < 2b}}
\stmt{2 \cdot k!}
\reason{<}{#{2 < k+1} since #{k \geq 4}}
\stmt{(k+1) \cdot k!}
\reason{=}{definition of factorial}
\stmt{(k+1)!}

Thus, by transitivity of #{<}, we have #{2^{k+1} < (k+1)!}, which is what we wished to show.
}
}
